<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julfar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julfar was a medieval port on the northern coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> peninsula, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras-al-khaimah">Ras al-Khaimah</a> in the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a>. From the 14th to the 17th century it was a centre of pearling and of trade between the Gulf, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, and it was briefly held by the Portuguese. It declined as Ras al-Khaimah grew nearby in the 18th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Julfar replaced the fortified port of Kush, founded in the 5th century, whose lagoon silted up; Kush was abandoned only gradually, in the 13th century. The new town grew on two sandbanks protecting a lagoon south-west of the palm gardens, Nudud to the south, probably settled first, and Mataf to the north. A city wall about 1.5 metres thick closed its three landward sides, and the Wadi Sur, a wall 7 kilometres long, 5 metres high and 2 metres thick with a ditch and watchtowers, protected about 85 per cent of its farmland between the shore and the mountains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first settlement, of the early to mid 14th century, was a cluster of palm-frond huts whose people fished, probably pearled and farmed the interior. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">Mud brick</a> and stone buildings appeared by the end of that century, and between the early 15th and early 16th centuries Julfar became a planned town with a large mosque, a fort and dense streets of mudbrick, stone and coral houses stretching 5 kilometres along the coast, with perhaps up to 70,000 people by the 16th century. The navigator Ahmad ibn Majid was born there in 1432.</p><p>Its growth is linked to the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>, which had no fresh water and little food and drew pearls, food and water from Julfar; Hormuz probably ruled the town directly in the 1520s and is credited with rebuilding the mosque in stone. Writing in 1518, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duarte-barbosa">Duarte Barbosa</a> described its great pearl fishery, whose pearls the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> of Ormus carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The Portuguese sacked Julfar in 1621, and the fort they built there fell to Nasir bin Murshid, the first Yaruba imam of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The British archaeologist Beatrice di Cardi identified the site in 1968, noting a stretch of coast littered with Ming porcelain. An Iraqi team first dug at Nudud in 1973/4, and John Hansman investigated it in 1977/8. Between 1988 and 1994 teams from the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, invited by the Ras Al Khaimah Department of Antiquities and Museums, made it one of the most excavated sites in the Gulf. The coarse Julfar ware pottery made inland is found widely around the Gulf and the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Julfar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Qawasim, an Arab tribe on the northern coast of the Oman peninsula, established themselves at Ras al-Khaimah, near the old port of <b>Julfar</b>, in the early 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-ras-al-khaimah">Sheikhdom of Ras al-Khaimah</a></li></ul></section>
